    "narrative": "The offensive engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on **Mickey Moniak** today, our highest-rated start at 75.75. **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a perfect storm of environmental and situational factors: a significant park boost in today\u2019s venue, a favorable dynamic platoon advantage, and the fact that the opposing pitcher, Gusto, exhibits a slow delivery that grants **Mickey Moniak** a 2% \"stolen base bonus\"\u2014a critical edge given our internal weighting on speed. Following closely, **Josh Naylor** (67.78) anchors our infield, buoyed by a strong spot in the #3 hole and a soft xERA matchup against Walbert Ure\u00f1a. While **Kyle Karros** (56.78) and **Alex Freeland** (56.85) provide solid mid-tier production, we are exercising caution with **Dalton Rushing** (54.55) and **Austin Riley** (51.91), as their utility is slightly dampened by the presence of elite holding pitchers on the mound, which suppresses our stolen base potential.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Travis Bazzana**, whose 45.98 score narrowly missed the cut. While **Travis Bazzana** gained significant value from a projected #1 lineup spot, the optimizer ultimately favored the higher raw floors of our locked-in starters like **Brice Turang** and **Cole Young**. We have opted to keep **Edwin Arroyo** on the bench, as his sub-40 efficiency score against the formidable Misiorowski makes him a clear candidate to preserve our daily game caps. **Vinnie Pasquantino** remains sidelined on the IL, further restricting our flexibility. We are proceeding with \"Lineup Pending\" status for a significant portion of our roster, most notably **Dalton Rushing**, **Josh Naylor**, **Cole Young**, **Alex Freeland**, and **Austin Riley**; should these positions shift downward in the final batting orders, we may see further volatility in our projected offensive output.",
